oxed - SOFTWARE REQUIREMENTS SPECIFICATION
Document: OXED-SRS-001
Revision: 0.3-draft
Status: DRAFT
Date: 2026-11-21

1. SCOPE

1.1 oxed is a keyboard-first structural text editor written in OxCaml.
1.2 Its command language combines ed's economy with Sam's
    compositional structural regular expressions.
1.3 The editor operates on Unicode text ranges rather than
    treating lines as the fundamental editing objects.
1.4 Numbered output, browsing, and optional mouse interaction
    augment the command language without replacing it.
1.5 Tree-sitter and LSP are optional future extensions, not
    dependencies of the editing core.

2. CONVENTIONS

2.1 MUST, SHOULD, and MAY follow RFC 2119 and RFC 8174.
2.2 Each REQ identifier is unique and stable across revisions.
2.3 Every MUST requirement requires verification evidence.
2.4 Half-open ranges use [start, stop) notation.
2.5 Requirements describe target behavior unless verified in
    Section 7.

3. ARCHITECTURE

3.1 Main       Application lifecycle and resource cleanup
3.2 App        Event routing, command input, and rendering
3.3 Terminal   Lambda-term input and display
3.4 Workspace  Files, buffers, and active document
3.5 Editor     Zed-backed Unicode text, cursors, and editing
3.6 Ed         Sam-style addressing and command interpretation
3.7 Syntax     Optional syntax parsing and overlays
3.8 Language   Optional LSP processes and language services

3.9 Ed owns command selection state (dot).
3.10 Editor owns the authoritative text buffer.
3.11 Command and visual selections MUST use compatible ranges.
3.12 Syntax and Language MUST NOT be required for basic editing.

4. FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS

REQ-001 Editor MUST own authoritative text and editing cursor state.

REQ-002 Editing operations MUST use a common, undoable
        transaction pathway.

REQ-003 Ed MUST support ., $, absolute and relative line
        addresses, marks, searches, and compound ranges.

REQ-004 Ed MUST support p, n, a, i, c, d, s, x, y, g, v,
        w, q, and u.

REQ-005 An optional syntax extension MAY provide @ selection
        addresses. Such support is not required by the core.

REQ-006 Workspace MUST support opening, editing, and saving files.

REQ-007 Terminal MUST support keyboard input and resizing.
        Mouse interaction MAY be implemented separately.

REQ-008 Terminal MUST restore its state after normal termination
        and recoverable failures.

REQ-009 If a syntax extension is enabled, its parse tree MUST
        remain derived from the authoritative editor buffer.

REQ-010 If LSP is enabled, Language MUST support versioned
        document synchronization.

REQ-011 Stale LSP results MUST NOT overwrite newer text state.

REQ-012 Text ranges MUST preserve Unicode scalar boundaries.
        UTF-8 offsets and, when LSP is enabled, UTF-16 offsets
        MUST be converted without corrupting buffer positions.

REQ-013 File operations MUST preserve buffer integrity on failure.

REQ-014 Release builds MUST NOT contain reachable TODO stubs.

REQ-015 Ed selections MUST be represented as half-open ranges,
        independently of line-oriented addressing.

REQ-016 Structural expressions MUST support composable regular
        expression operations over selected text ranges.

REQ-017 The n command MUST display source line numbers without
        external filters or modification of buffer contents.

REQ-018 The command prompt MUST accept Unicode input and MUST
        distinguish command input from buffer editing.

REQ-019 If mouse support is implemented, it MUST be disabled
        by default and configurable through :set mouse and
        :set nomouse.

REQ-020 Invalid addresses MUST NOT modify dot or buffer contents.

REQ-021 Viewport scrolling MUST NOT implicitly change dot.

REQ-022 The editor MUST remain usable without Tree-sitter,
        language grammars, or an LSP server.

REQ-023 Command parsing MUST distinguish address selection
        from command execution.

5. COMMAND LANGUAGE

5.1 General

5.1.1 Ed provides the command interpreter.
5.1.2 The command prompt uses the Greek lambda: λ
5.1.3 Commands operate on dot unless an explicit address
      supplies another selection.
5.1.4 Successful addressed operations update dot according
      to the command's selection semantics.
5.1.5 The command language SHOULD preserve Sam's compositional
      model wherever practical.

5.2 Addresses

.           Current selection (dot)
$           Empty selection at end-of-file
0           Beginning-of-file address
n           Absolute line address, when used as an address
+           Next line relative to dot
-           Previous line relative to dot
/re/        Forward regular-expression search
?re?        Backward regular-expression search
a,b         Range spanning two addresses
,           Entire buffer

5.3 Commands

p           Print selected text
n           Print selected text with source line numbers
x/re/       Iterate over matching substrings
y/re/       Iterate over text between matches
g/re/       Execute when selection matches
v/re/       Execute when selection does not match
s/re/t/     Substitute text
a           Append text
i           Insert text
c           Change selection
d           Delete selection
u           Undo
w           Write file
q           Quit

5.4 Numbered Output

5.4.1 n is a deliberate extension to Sam.
5.4.2 n MUST display the original source line numbers.
5.4.3 Numbering MUST NOT alter the underlying text.
5.4.4 n SHOULD support ordinary addresses, ranges, and
      structural selections.
5.4.5 Sam's historical file-listing command n is not retained
      under that spelling.

5.5 Structural Expressions

5.5.1 x and y MUST operate on matching text ranges.
5.5.2 Structural commands SHOULD permit nesting.
5.5.3 Regular expressions remain the primary selection engine.
5.5.4 No language parser is required for structural editing.
5.5.5 Compound modifications MUST preserve address validity
      and MUST NOT partially corrupt the buffer.

5.6 Editor Settings

:set          Display settings
:set mouse    Enable mouse interaction, when supported
:set nomouse  Disable mouse interaction, when supported

5.6.1 Settings MUST remain separate from structural commands.
5.6.2 Unsupported settings MUST report an explicit error.
5.6.3 Keyboard operation MUST NOT depend on mouse availability.

6. IMPLEMENTATION CONSTRAINTS

6.1 OxCaml with Base, Core, and Core_unix.
6.2 Zed provides Unicode text editing and rope infrastructure.
6.3 Lambda-term provides terminal input and rendering.
6.4 Lwt coordinates asynchronous operations.
6.5 Ed implements the command parser and range interpreter.
6.6 Regular-expression selection MUST NOT require Tree-sitter.
6.7 Tree-sitter and LSP integration are deferred and optional.
6.8 Zed refers to the OCaml library, not the Rust Zed editor.
6.9 The editor SHOULD minimize mandatory dependencies.
6.10 The initial interface MUST remain keyboard-operable.

